---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Services"
  description="A fixed thirty-day engagement to map and automate manual work, fixed-scope builds, and embedded engineering from 2027 — Devin MacArthur, Portland, Oregon"
>
  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="hero-topo section bg-gradient-to-b from-dark-bg via-dark-surface to-dark-bg">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl">
        <h1 class="mb-6 text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-5xl">What I do, and what it costs</h1>
        <p class="text-lg text-text-secondary">
          Two things now, a third starting in 2027. The prices are fixed because I would rather
          argue about scope once, on paper, than about hours every month.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Diagnostic -->
  <section class="section border-t border-dark-border">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl">
        <h2 class="mb-3 text-3xl font-bold">The thirty days</h2>
        <p class="mb-8 text-lg text-text-secondary">
          <strong class="text-text-primary">$12,000. Half when we start, half when I hand it over.</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="space-y-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
          <p>
            The first week I talk to the people who do the work. Not managers, or not only
            managers: the person who exports the report on Monday, the person who re-keys the
            form, the person who knows which spreadsheet is the real one. Five to eight
            conversations, an hour each. I also ask for read-only access to the systems they
            mention, so I can see what they're describing instead of imagining it.
          </p>
          <p>
            By the end of the second week you get a map of the five processes that cost the most
            hours, with the hours counted, the places errors get in, and what each one is costing
            you. We pick one together. I write the scope for it on a single page and we both sign
            it, because the most common way these things go wrong is the scope growing quietly.
          </p>
          <p>
            Weeks three and four I build it and put it into production. That word means
            something specific to me: it runs on a schedule, it logs what it did and what it
            couldn't do, it fails in a way someone will notice, and there is a written procedure
            for the day it breaks. I train whoever will own it, usually in half an hour, and I
            leave a twelve-month plan for the other four processes: which to do next, which to buy
            software for instead, and which to leave alone.
          </p>
          <p>
            What I need from you is one person who can make a decision within two days, about six
            hours of your staff's time across the month, and the access I mentioned. Write access
            only to the one thing we're automating, and you can revoke it whenever you like.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="mt-10">
          <a href="/contact/" class="btn btn-primary">Ask about the thirty days</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Fixed-scope builds -->
  <section class="section bg-dark-surface">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl">
        <h2 class="mb-3 text-3xl font-bold">A single build</h2>
        <p class="mb-8 text-lg text-text-secondary">
          <strong class="text-text-primary">$5,000 to $15,000, quoted after a call.</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="space-y-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
          <p>
            For organizations that already know which process they want gone and don't need the
            thirty days to find it. The things I get asked for most are intake forms that file
            themselves and their attachments into the right place, a nightly reconciliation that
            names every mismatch between two systems, a morning report that assembles itself from
            three sources, and scanned documents turned into records without anyone retyping.
            Each one ships the same way the thirty-day build does: scheduled, logged, documented,
            handed over.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Embedded -->
  <section class="section border-t border-dark-border">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl">
        <h2 class="mb-3 text-3xl font-bold">Embedded, from early 2027</h2>
        <p class="mb-8 text-lg text-text-secondary">
          <strong class="text-text-primary">$25,000 to $35,000 a month, six months minimum, about half my time.</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="space-y-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
          <p>
            This is the engagement I actually want to do, and I can't do it properly until I'm
            doing this full time, which I expect to be by early 2027. I sit inside your organization,
            on site or remote depending on the work, and take ownership of the manual work as a
            whole rather than one process at a time. The first ninety days go to the worst
            process. After that, one more into production every month or six weeks.
          </p>
          <p>
            Every thirty days we agree in writing what ships next. Every week you get a page from
            me saying what shipped, what's stuck, and what I'm doing about it. Everything is
            documented as it's built, so if I got hit by a bus your team could keep running what
            exists. When it ends, the last month is handover.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you want to be first in line for this, say so now. I'm keeping a short list.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Data -->
  <section class="section bg-dark-surface">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl">
        <h2 class="mb-6 text-3xl font-bold">Your data</h2>
        <div class="space-y-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
          <p>
            I've written a one-page data-handling sheet that I'll send before we sign anything.
            The short version: your data stays in your systems; anything sensitive is processed
            on your machines or on hardware I own, not on a hosted model; if a hosted model is
            the right tool for something, it's a provider with a data-processing agreement, named
            in the scope; named accounts only; credentials rotated back to you at the end; my
            working copies deleted and confirmed in writing. I don't train anything on your data
            and I don't reuse one client's work for another.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CTA -->
  <section class="section border-t border-dark-border">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl">
        <h2 class="mb-6 text-3xl font-bold">Questions</h2>
        <p class="mb-8 text-lg text-text-secondary">
          The fastest way to find out if this fits is a thirty-minute call. Email me with the
          process in mind and a couple of times that work.
        </p>
        <a href="/contact/" class="btn btn-primary">Contact</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
